<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-verifying-metric-properties" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Verifying metric properties
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Determine whether or not the given function is a metric.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> d \colon \R \times \R \to \R </m> be the Euclidean metric on the real numbers <m> \R </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } }
        </md>
        
        for all real numbers <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \R </m>. Verify that <m> d </m> is a metric on <m> \R </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        For all real numbers <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \R </m>, if <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } = \abs{ 0 } = 0
        </md>.
        
        Conversely, if <m> x_{ 1 } \neq x_{ 2 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } = \max \of{ \set{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } } - \min \of{ \set{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } } \gt 0
        </md>.
        
        Since <m> d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0 </m> if and only if <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } </m>, <m> d </m> is non-degenerate.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Since
        
        <md>
          d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } = \abs{ x_{ 2 } - x_{ 1 } } = d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } }
        </md>
        
        for all real numbers <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>, <m> d </m> is symmetric.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Finally, consider three points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } \in \R </m>. If <m> x_{ 1 } \geq x_{ 2 } \geq x_{ 3 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } } \amp = x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } = x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } + \abs{ x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 } } = d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        If <m> x_{ 1 } \geq x_{ 2 } \geq x_{ 3 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } } \amp = x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } = x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } - \abs{ x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 } } \leq d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        If <m> x_{ 2 } \geq x_{ 1 } \geq x_{ 3 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } } \amp = x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } = x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = -\abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } + \abs{ x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 } } \leq d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        If <m> x_{ 2 } \geq x_{ 3 } \geq x_{ 1 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } } \amp = x_{ 3 } - x_{ 1 } = x_{ 3 } - x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 } - x_{ 1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } - \abs{ x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 } } \leq d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        If <m> x_{ 3 } \geq x_{ 1 } \geq x_{ 2 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } } \amp = x_{ 3 } - x_{ 1 } = x_{ 3 } - x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 } - x_{ 1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = -\abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } + \abs{ x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 } } \leq d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Finally, if <m> x_{ 3 } \geq x_{ 2 } \geq x_{ 1 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 3 } } \amp = x_{ 3 } - x_{ 1 } = x_{ 3 } - x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 } - x_{ 1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } + \abs{ x_{ 2 } - x_{ 3 } } = d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        In any case, since <m> d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + d \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>, <m> d </m> satisfies the triangle inequality.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Since <m> d </m> is non-degenerate, symmetric, and satisfies the triangle inequality, it is a metric on the real numbers <m> \R </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \delta_{ X } \colon X \times X \to \R </m> be the discrete metric on a set <m> X </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } =
            \begin{cases}
              0 \amp x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } \\
              1 \amp x_{ 1 } \neq x_{ 2 }
            \end{cases}
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. Verify that <m> \delta_{ X } </m> is a metric on <m> X </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        For all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>, if <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0
        </md>.
        
        Conversely, if <m> x_{ 1 } \neq x_{ 2 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 1 \neq 0
        </md>.
        
        Since <m> \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0 </m> if and only if <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } </m>, <m> \delta_{ X } </m> is non-degenerate.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Consider two points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. If <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } </m>, then <m> x_{ 2 } = x_{ 1 } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0 = \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } }
        </md>.
        
        Conversely, if <m> x_{ 1 } \neq x_{ 2 } </m>, then <m> x_{ 2 } \neq x_{ 1 } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 1 = \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } }
        </md>.
        
        In either case, since <m> \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } } </m>, <m> \delta_{ X } </m> is symmetric.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Finally, consider three points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } \in X </m>. If <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 3 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } = 0 \leq \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } 
        </md>,
        
        since <m> \delta_{ X } </m> takes only non-negative values. On the other hand, suppose that <m> x_{ 1 } \neq x_{ 3 } </m>. Then it is not the case that <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } </m> and <m> x_{ 2 } = x_{ 3 } </m>, and so at least one of <m> \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } </m> and <m> \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m> is <m> 1 </m>. We conclude that
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } = 1 \leq \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } 
        </md>.
        
        In either case, since <m> \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>, <m> \delta_{ X } </m> satisfies the triangle inequality.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Since <m> \delta_{ X } </m> is non-degenerate, symmetric, and satisfies the triangle inequality, it is a metric on <m> X </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \rho \colon \R \times \R \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 }
        </md>.
        
        Determine whether or not <m> \rho </m> is a metric on the real numbers <m> \R </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We note that
        
        <md>
          \rho \of{ 0 , 1 } = 0 - 1 = -1 \neq 1 = 1 - 0 = \rho \of{ 1 , 0 }
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> \rho </m> is not symmetric. Since <m> \rho </m> is not symmetric, it is not a metric on the real numbers <m> \R </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
